Chapter 1: System Introduction

1.1 Introduction
   Provide a brief overview of the software application, its purpose, and the problem it addresses.

1.2 System Objectives
   List the key objectives the system aims to achieve (e.g., improve user productivity, automate a business process, etc.).

1.3 Background and Problem Statement
   Describe the context in which the system will be used and clearly state the problem that motivated the development.

1.4 Operational Environment
   Summarize the hardware, operating system, network, and any external interfaces that the system will operate within.

[Figure Description: High‑level overview diagram of the system architecture – textual description of components and their interactions]

---

Chapter 2: System Analysis – Requirements & Modeling

2.1 Software Requirement Specification (SRS)
   • Functional Requirements – detailed list of functions the system must perform.
   • Non‑Functional Requirements – performance, security, usability, reliability, etc.
   • Constraints – hardware, software, regulatory, and any other constraints.

2.2 Data Flow Diagrams (DFDs)
   Level‑0 DFD – description of the system as a single process with external entities.
   Level‑1 DFD – description of major sub‑processes and data stores.
   Level‑2 DFD – further decomposition of complex sub‑processes.
   [Figure Description: DFD Level‑2 for "User Management" – textual steps and data flow]

2.3 Entity‑Relationship (E‑R) / Class Diagrams
   • E‑R Diagram – description of entities, attributes, and relationships for the database.
   • Class Diagram – description of major classes, attributes, methods, and associations.
   [Figure Description: Class diagram for the core business domain – textual representation of classes and relationships]

2.4 Data Dictionary
   Provide a tabular description of each data element used in the system (name, type, length, description, source).

---

Chapter 3: System Design – Architecture

3.1 Module Structure
   List each software module, its purpose, and a brief description of its internal structure.

3.2 Database Schema
   • Tables – names, primary keys, foreign keys, and integrity constraints.
   • Sample schema description (textual representation of tables and columns).

3.3 Process Logic Flowcharts
   For each major module, describe the logical flow of operations.
   [Figure Description: Flowchart for "Login Process" – textual step‑by‑step flow]

3.4 User Interface Layouts
   • Initial screen mock‑up – textual description of layout, fields, and controls.
   • Navigation flow – description of how users move between screens.
   [Figure Description: UI layout of the dashboard – textual overview of panels and widgets]

---

Chapter 4: Implementation & Testing – Validation

4.1 Platform, Coding Environment, and Tools
   • Programming language(s) used.
   • Development framework(s) and libraries.
   • IDE, version‑control system, and other utilities.

4.2 Implementation Details
   Provide brief notes on important implementation choices, algorithms, and any third‑party components.

4.3 Test Cases
   • Unit Test Cases – description of individual component tests and expected results.
   • Integration Test Cases – description of interactions between modules.
   • System Test Cases – end‑to‑end scenarios covering functional and non‑functional requirements.

4.4 Test Results
   Summarize the actual validation outcomes for each test case (e.g., Pass/Fail, observations).
   Include any screenshots or logs as textual descriptions where appropriate.

[Figure Description: Sample test report table – textual representation of test case ID, description, expected result, actual result]

